   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.1. Increased capacity of the ILO constituents to identify current skills mismatches and anticipate future skill needs</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">ILO&apos;s methodologies on skills needs assessment, namely Skills Technology Foresight (STF) and Skills for Trade and Economic Diversification (STED) were adopted and pilot tested by ILO partners in two selected sectors, as a means to promoting industry-led TVET system. The ILO/Skills for Prosperity Programme (SfP) in Malaysia provided the following capacity building programs, contributing to strengthening the national labour market and skills needs anticipation system. The programmes were undertaken in cooperation with the ITCILO.

-Sectoral Approaches to Skills Development, in which 5 government officials and one representative of an employer&apos;s organisation participated.

-Institutional Capacity Building for Effective Labour Market Information System (LMIS), in which 6 government officials participated.

-A tailor-made e-learning course on Introduction into LMIS and Skills Demand Assessment, where 99 officials from government agencies involved in the development of the Labour Market Information Analytics Planform (LMIAP)under the 12th Malaysian Plan participated.

The SfP Southeast Asia organised the following regional webinars, in which ILO constituents in Malaysia participated.

-Promoting industry-led skills and TVET systems in Asia: The Role of Sector Skills Councils.</narrative>
   </description>
